WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:05.890
I do think we are at an inflection point

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.770
for brain-computer interfaces,

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.260
what I would call a Kitty Hawk moment.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.080
And I think just as we saw
after the Wright brothers sent

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.260
that plane into the air,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.430
we're gonna see an explosion
of advanced development

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.563
of these technologies.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.980
Over the past few years,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.940
the prospect of linking our
brains directly with computers

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.230
has given rise to the
beginnings of a new industry.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.120
Dozens of startups are popping up,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.680
creating products to help people

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.840
with mental and physical disabilities,

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.680
revolutionize the way we
interact with technology,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.480
and even alter the
functioning of our own brains.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.380
We essentially open the door to the access

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.290
of our minds by technology.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.980
So we're talking about the
changing of foundations

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.430
of what it means to be human.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.190
How did we get here?

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.810
How well does all this
stuff actually work?

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.450
And if this technology lives up

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.043
to its transformational
potential, are we ready for it?

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.090
So a brain-computer interface is a device

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:16.850
that interfaces the brain
with the outside world.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.070
It could be a computer,
it could be a machine.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:23.220
And these brain-computer
interfaces, or BCIs,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.710
could be inserted inside the brain.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.930
So those are invasive BCIs,

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.090
or they could be wearables
that you would wear

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.563
on the top of your head.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:39.040
BCIs can record the activity of the brain,

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.740
the firing of individual neurons.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.310
Obviously, if you take
information out of the brain,

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.340
you can in principle
decode what that means.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.630
And just like there was a
transition in technology

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.560
from the PC to the smartphone,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.230
it is possible, I would say likely,

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.830
that the next transition
will be from a device

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.830
that you have in your pocket

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.780
to a device that you have in your head.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:06.983
If you've heard

00:02:06.983 --> 00:02:10.460
of one brain-computer interface,
it's probably this one.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.150
All right, welcome to the
Neuralink product demo.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.930
I'm really excited to
show you what we've got.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.600
I think it's gonna blow your mind.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.710
When Elon speaks, we all listen,

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:23.410
that's just life in the 21st century.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.160
But Neuralink and its competitors

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.980
didn't come out of nowhere.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.790
They're built on decades
of work by neuroscientists

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.620
attempting to understand and interpret

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.070
what's going on in the brain.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.730
One of those neuroscientists,
and a key player

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:42.010
in the development of BCIs, is
this guy, Dr. Leigh Hochberg.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.270
I'm the director of the
BrainGate pilot clinical trials.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:51.270
The BrainGate pilot clinical
trials are seeking to determine

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.550
the safety and feasibility
of allowing somebody

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.740
with paralysis to control
an external device

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.120
simply by thinking.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.250
The origins of this field
can be traced easily back

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.343
to the late 1950s, early 1960s.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.260
Dr. Robert Heath has implanted electrodes,

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.420
which cause no pain, in the
brains of these monkeys.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.130
Researchers were placing single
electrodes into the brain

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.100
of animals in a research laboratory,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.957
asking the fairly
straightforward question of,

00:03:22.957 --> 00:03:25.910
"How does that neuron in
that part of the brain

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.693
relate to the control
of voluntary movement?

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.130
And then in the late 1990s,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.680
here at Brown University,

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.000
John Donoghue, professor of neuroscience,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.500
did an experiment in
the research laboratory,

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.880
which was to ask a non-human
primate to play a video game.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.480
And the researchers begin to build a map

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.410
between the brain activity
and the movement of the hand.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.590
After building that map,

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:51.960
that program would be able to predict

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.183
where the animal's hand
actually was in space.

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.430
You could then play a
small trick on the animal,

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.230
which is to disconnect the joystick.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.110
And at that moment,

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.620
the joystick is no longer
controlling the cursor

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:05.900
on the screen,

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.040
the neural activity itself

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.550
is controlling the cursor movement.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.620
So we begin to describe that as a cursor

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.110
that's moving under neural power alone.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.810
And that was really a breakthrough moment.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.050
Within a few years,

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.550
researchers were trying out
the same thing in humans.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.777
So in the spring of 2004,

00:04:24.777 --> 00:04:27.253
the BrainGate clinical
trials were launched.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.220
The technology begins with a sensor,

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:32.860
or an array as we call it,

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.230
that's placed in the brain.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.890
The sensor itself is about
four by four millimeters,

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.530
the size of a baby aspirin.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:41.570
And when tapped into the top of the brain,

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.960
it has the ability to record
from individual neurons.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.550
So that when somebody thinks
about moving their own hand,

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.630
we can allow them to control
a cursor on a computer screen

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.373
or some other assistive technology.

00:04:54.373 --> 00:04:56.612
What do you want to do first?

00:04:56.612 --> 00:04:58.852
[Study Participant] I'd
like to draw a circle.

00:04:58.852 --> 00:05:00.243
You're gonna draw a circle?

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:04.890
Excellent.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.500
That incredible first
participant was able to think

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.167
about opening and closing
a prosthetic hand.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.210
And which was,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:20.043
I think he said out loud what
everybody else was thinking.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.750
The third participant was
an extraordinary woman

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:28.460
who had had a stroke in the brainstem.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.050
She could feel everything,
she could hear everything,

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.300
but she couldn't move
and she couldn't speak.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.593
And she said she wanted to take a drink.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:39.410
So we brought in a robotic arm.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:42.810
She was thinking about
reaching and grasping

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.300
that thermos of coffee.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.000
As she did that, the robot arm moved out,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.920
grabbed that thermos of coffee.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.740
And then she took a sip of cinnamon latte

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.160
and that was the first
time in nearly 15 years

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.373
that she was able to do so
solely of her own volition.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:00.667
Over the 17 years

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:02.580
of the BrainGate trials,

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.630
the technology has come a long way,

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.990
particularly as advances
in machine learning

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.170
have made strides helping to decode data

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:10.453
recorded from the brain.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.240
Recently, they were even
able to have a paralyzed man

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.400
imagine the motion of handwriting

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.573
and translate that into text
on a screen with 94% accuracy.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:24.480
But clinical trials in university labs,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.560
they're really just a first step.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.790
At some point there needs to
be an off-ramp to a company

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:33.360
who is then gonna focus
on developing what,

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.800
as an academic, a word that
I don't use that often,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.100
to make a product.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.810
It needs to be submitted
for regulatory approval

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.720
and then it needs to be
marketed and supported

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:42.833
and made available.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:46.890
When we first started this company,

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:47.970
people almost kind of took this

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.820
as just kind of almost
crazy science fiction.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:54.260
And I think we're getting
past the believability factor.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:57.420
We're starting to see what
was originally kind of

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.260
research technology take
flight in the clinical domain.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.580
And I think we're gonna
see many, many more

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.453
of these type of technologies.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.090
Here in St. Louis,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.370
a company called Neurolutions recently got

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:14.020
one of the first FDA
approvals ever for a BCI.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.010
In this case, a noninvasive EEG headset

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:19.453
that reads the electrical
activity of the brain.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.310
The measurements aren't as precise

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.560
as with an invasive system,
like the one BrainGate uses,

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.960
but they work for Neurolutions' purpose:

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.300
helping stroke victims
to reconnect their mind

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.120
with a paralyzed limb.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.730
All right, so we're just gonna test

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.440
the headset real quick.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.890
All connections good. Nice and relaxed.

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:41.310
My name's Mark Forrest.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:43.810
I lived in St. Louis all my life

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.490
and turns out I had a stroke

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.800
and I was pretty much
paralyzed on my right side.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:51.980
A lot of people told me after six months

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:53.690
that I was pretty much done

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.740
and I was gonna have hardly any movement.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:00.010
When somebody has a stroke,
a part of their brain dies.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:02.090
For instance, the right side is injured,

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.513
the left side can't move.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:06.980
Now, but if you talk to these patients,

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:08.240
they can imagine moving.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:09.700
They can try to move.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.440
They just can't actually
execute that movement.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.610
We know that 90% of those motor fibers

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:17.710
that control opening and closing
that hand are on that side

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.030
where the injury occurred.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.070
What we found is that there's actually 10%

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:23.660
on the healthy side, on
the other side of the brain

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:26.447
that controls thought of,
"I want to move my hand."

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:28.840
And so what we did is,

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.850
we created a brain-computer interface

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:34.770
that really has three parts
to it: a wearable headset,

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:38.590
a robotic exoskeleton, and a
tablet that walks them through

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:40.310
how to use the system.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:41.143
All right, let's see.

00:08:41.143 --> 00:08:44.120
So let's do 30 seconds rest
and then I'm gonna hit Next

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.020
and we'll see what control you'll get.

00:08:46.863 --> 00:08:49.670
At the beginning of the
program it'll tells you

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:51.380
not to move your hand,

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.720
but think about moving your hand,

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.447
and that's what you have to do.

00:08:55.447 --> 00:08:57.690
Now just imagine.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.250
It's picking up that intention to move

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.940
on the uninjured side of the brain.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:04.410
It's converting that
intention to a movement

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:05.413
of the exoskeleton.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:12.070
As the person continues
to use it over time,

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.080
it's essentially leading
to a rewiring of the brain.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:16.600
There's this kind of neuroscience notion

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.490
of what fires together, wires together.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:21.110
So when they're generating
those brain signals to move

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.490
and they're getting that
feedback from that wearable,

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:26.210
that's leading to new
connections forming in the brain

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:28.970
that allows, eventually, that
uninjured side of the brain

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:31.120
to take over control
of the paralyzed limb.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.200
My movement was just this,
that's all I could do.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.950
Look at how much difference
I've made - progressed,

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:40.650
using that machine.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:44.580
I have arm movement. I
have finger movement.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:46.910
I have wrist movement.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:48.360
It got me motivated for,

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.020
I actually built my own fishing boat.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:54.110
My eldest son said, it's gonna
sink. It's not gonna float.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:55.730
My best friend told me it was gonna sink

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.060
and not gonna float.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.120
Well, I've proved them all wrong.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.437
The lord works in mysterious ways,

00:10:01.437 --> 00:10:02.660
[expletive deleted]
happens and there's nothing

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:03.830
you can do about it.

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:05.080
So you just make it work.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.150
Companies like Neurolutions
are bringing BCIs

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.060
into the realm of healthcare,

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:16.410
but that's kind of just
the tip of the iceberg.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:20.910
Tech companies are starting
to get in on the action too

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.210
and they've got a whole
different set of uses in mind

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.150
for these technologies.

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:25.983
At the end of the day,

00:10:25.983 --> 00:10:29.320
the only thing that a
computer or a smartphone does

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:32.300
is connect you to the 'net.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.640
Well, with a BCI, you'll be able to do

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:36.900
this much faster and easier.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.710
The leaders of Google and Facebook,

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:40.930
they expect in the future,

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:44.240
to be able to just
communicate directly with BCI.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.760
In the future we want to get to an input

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:49.940
where we can just think
something and it happens.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:51.980
And this also has
stimulated the generation

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.530
of many, many new companies,
neurotech companies,

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:57.993
probably more than 100
over the last year or so.

00:10:59.062 --> 00:10:59.895
One of those companies

00:10:59.895 --> 00:11:01.740
is French startup NextMind,

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.250
which is making a small wearable

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:05.490
that can detect the
firing of your neurons,

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:08.010
much like the Neurolutions headset.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.110
And you can actually buy
one from them right now

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.803
for about 400 bucks.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:15.410
We are a neurotech company using a device

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.270
that you can put on your head directly,

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:21.560
and that can allow you to
control interfaces directly

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:22.420
with your mind.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:23.530
Back of my head, like right here,

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.540
like the dome part of the head?

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:27.620
We enlisted some Quicktake producers

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:30.300
to take NextMind's
interface for a test drive.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:34.060
Comb the electrodes through
your hair to touch the scalp.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:36.250
Super cool. Okay.

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.070
We decode,

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:41.410
directly from your visual
cortex at the back of the head,

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.540
the fluctuations of
attention that you have

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:46.940
in the visual cortex when
you're focusing on something.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:49.840
So when you have a bunch
of objects on the screen,

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.100
those small triangles,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.620
we call them neurotags,

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:55.500
basically we can not only
know that you are looking

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.600
at one of them, but also
discriminate, very quickly,

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:01.360
whether you wanna push
this one or this one.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.582
Focusing on the center disc.

00:12:05.582 --> 00:12:07.640
Oh, my gosh.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:09.480
Oh, wow, okay.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.720
It's like the Force, you know?

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:13.460
Freaky.

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:15.500
The current product is aimed at developers

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:18.150
who want to build their
own mind-controlled apps,

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:20.200
but it does come with a few short demos,

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:24.090
like a game that's controlled
part manually, part mentally.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.290
Lead the little square guy
to the end of the level.

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:28.970
Help him out using your
mind to blow up enemies

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:31.170
and trigger mechanisms.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:32.170
This is quite fun.

00:12:34.065 --> 00:12:36.544
I blew up a enemy square.

00:12:36.544 --> 00:12:38.220
I don't know how I feel
about staring at enemies

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:41.731
to blow them up, this is
promoting some kind of violence.

00:12:41.731 --> 00:12:45.320
It's like your brain thinks
that you're able to do it

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:46.500
and it just does it.

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.693
So it feels, you know, like the future.

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:52.070
Bloomberg's beta testers
had a pretty good time

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:54.140
with NextMind's device overall,

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:55.730
but there was some disappointment

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:58.323
that it wasn't more, well, mind-read-y.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.147
At the moment, you can push
a button by staring at it,

00:13:02.147 --> 00:13:03.770
and that's about it.

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:05.630
It's like cool, but it's,

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:07.370
I just don't really know
how practical it is.

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:10.370
Honestly, my brain kinda hurts using this.

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:11.980
I'm a little afraid
about being that plugged

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:13.430
into a video game.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:17.223
That like, literally, if my
attention goes off of it,

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:19.640
it would break the game, right?

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:22.870
But of course, it's early
days for the company

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:25.130
and they ultimately see
this as a first step

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:28.420
towards new kinds of virtual interfaces.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:29.990
The thing we have in mind, really,

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:33.390
is to create this very
immersive experience

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:37.690
between your brain and the
virtual reality around you.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:41.000
So I am very convinced that
in a few years from now,

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:44.390
we're gonna have those cool,
like augmented reality glasses,

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:46.820
and there's gonna be sensors on them.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:49.000
And those sensors are
gonna be able to capture

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.710
some of your brain data
and allow you to basically

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.940
not have to move your hands in any way.

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:57.770
Just like be in direct connection

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.817
with the virtual objects surrounding you.

00:14:02.471 --> 00:14:04.720
NextMind sits at the consumer-grade end

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.020
of the emerging BCI market,

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.070
but it's just one of many
nascent companies in a field

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:12.200
with a wide range of different approaches,

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.033
intended applications, and funding levels.

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:18.790
At the other end of the
spectrum are companies

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:20.810
like Elon Musk's Neuralink,

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:23.300
spending many millions
of dollars in an attempt

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.963
to develop truly
game-changing neural hardware.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:33.493
Another such company is
Los Angeles-based Kernel.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.540
With over $100 million dollars in funding,

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:39.870
founder Bryan Johnson is setting
out to read brain activity

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.750
in ways we haven't quite seen before.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.080
The intuition people oftentimes associate

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.520
with interfaces is the
ability to control things.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.950
That is, change a channel
on TV, skip a song,

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.030
control a drone.

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:55.330
We believe the most promising potential

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:58.140
of these interfaces is measurement.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.270
When we have a wearable on our wrist,

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.850
we are aware of our
heartbeat and our steps

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:04.373
and our sleep stages.

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:06.770
If you went to the cardiologist,

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:10.640
you get your blood pressure
and EKG, cholesterol levels.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:14.640
One of the only things that
we cannot consistently measure

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:16.080
is our brains.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:20.673
We decided that the path forward
is to build new hardware.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.420
Kernel pitches its first product,

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:27.140
a $50,000 headset called Flow,

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:29.333
as a giant leap forward for the field.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:33.760
Most headsets on the market
right now use EEG technology

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:36.723
to get a fairly general
idea of your brain activity.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:40.963
The Flow offers a much more
detailed look inside your skull.

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:45.910
The way it works is we
shine light into the brain.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:47.420
That light goes into the brain.

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:50.520
Some of it bounces back and
then we use that information

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:52.350
to reconstruct the activity in the brain

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:54.710
in terms of blood oxygenation.

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:56.293
It's called spectroscopy.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:00.100
There has been neuroimaging
going on for decades.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:01.840
For example, fMRI.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:03.800
They provide great images of the brain,

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:06.350
they've just been going
on in very large systems.

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:08.960
They're a few million dollars
and they are room-sized.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:12.940
So what we've done is we've
shrunken a room-sized piece

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:15.180
of equipment into a helmet.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:18.100
And so now if you say a person
on average gets their brain

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:21.100
scanned, say, one time every five years,

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:23.220
you can now imagine a
scenario with our technology,

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.320
where a person is scanning their brain

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:26.893
one or multiple times a day.

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:30.260
Johnson imagines a deluge

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:33.220
of big brain data coming
from these devices,

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:36.100
helping science to tackle
all manner of hard questions

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.873
about the workings of the mind.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:40.340
It's a bit of a gamble though,

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:41.990
because we don't know exactly what

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.360
all that information will tell us.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:45.318
You know, it'll be TBD.

00:16:45.318 --> 00:16:46.518
We've built the technology out in front

00:16:46.518 --> 00:16:48.340
of the science, so the science does need

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:50.360
to be developed.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.940
The next step for us is
to get the first systems

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:54.500
in the hands of some
of the top researchers

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:56.440
in the entire world looking
at health and wellness

00:16:56.440 --> 00:17:00.700
of the brain, PTSD,
concussion, meditation,

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:03.410
the effects of caffeine on
the brain, psychedelics.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.410
And so it opens up all
kinds of possibilities

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:08.020
for mental health and
wellness, for performance,

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:11.110
for learning, for basically
anything that we do

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:12.003
with our minds.

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:17.280
As varied as the current crop of BCIs is,

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:19.590
most focus on the same thing:

00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:22.210
reading the activity of the
brain and translating that

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:23.623
into useful information.

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:27.870
But there's another
frontier in our attempts

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:29.760
to access the brain that comes with

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:32.220
more troubling implications:

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.420
not just reading from the brain,

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:37.543
but actually writing to,
or directly influencing it.

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:40.660
In a sense, this is already being done,

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:42.850
with devices like deep brain stimulators,

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:45.030
which send electrical
impulses into the brain

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.453
to treat movement
disorders like Parkinson's.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.240
But here at Columbia University,

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.450
neurobiologist Rafael Yuste
is proving that it's possible

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:55.280
to go much further,

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:58.233
to actually change the
brain's perception of reality.

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:01.590
So what we've done with mice is,

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:04.320
we've used a laser to measure the activity

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:08.210
of the visual cortex while the
mouse is looking at an image

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:13.210
and we train the mouse to lick
whenever he sees that image.

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:14.630
That's the first step.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:19.660
We can also use a second laser
to activate neurons at will,

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:23.740
a little bit as if we were
playing the piano with the brain.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:27.700
So we actually activate
the neurons that correspond

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:32.350
to a particular image, and
the mouse behaves exactly

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:36.380
the same as if he were seeing that image.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:38.370
So the mouse licks,

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.200
even though we're not
showing him the image,

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.110
we're just activating these neurons.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:45.050
So in a way we're sort of
taking control of his perception

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:48.230
and making him believe
that he's seeing something

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:49.130
that is not there.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:53.420
Now, what can be done in animals today

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:55.210
could be done in humans tomorrow.

00:18:55.210 --> 00:18:59.030
And we became concerned
that neurotechnology

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:00.423
should be regulated.

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:02.253
To that end,

00:19:02.253 --> 00:19:05.340
Yuste and a group of fellow
neuroscientists put together

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.070
a list of guidelines that they hope

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:10.700
will curb the dystopian
potential of the BCI.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:15.113
So we're proposing five new
human rights, neuro-rights:

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:19.103
the right to our mental privacy;

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:22.363
the right to our own identity;

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.830
the right to our own decision-making,

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:30.140
to prevent interference that
could change our behavior;

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:33.570
The fourth one has to do
with cognitive augmentation,

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.210
to prevent a situation where
people that have more resources

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:40.390
in certain countries,
will become augmented,

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:42.790
and become like super race of humans

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:45.080
versus the rest of the people.

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:47.900
There's already companies, like Neuralink,

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:52.130
whose purpose is to
mentally augment humans;

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:53.210
And the final right,

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:57.110
protection against bias in
artificial intelligence.

00:19:57.110 --> 00:20:00.040
It would be terrible if we augment humans,

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:02.600
but actually bring into their brains

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:05.473
a lot of biases that are
carried out by algorithms.

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:10.570
Given all those potential problems,

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:12.590
do you think neurotechnology ends up

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:14.933
being a net positive or a negative?

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:19.640
Oh, I'm very bullish about
BCIs and neurotechnology.

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:24.640
I think this is gonna be a
tremendous force of progress.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:28.920
Neurotechnologies are always neutral.

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:30.750
You can use them for good or for bad.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:32.050
We always have the duty

00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:34.690
when we come up with new technology,

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:37.410
to use it responsibly
and put the guard rails

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:40.703
so that this technology
is used to help humanity.

00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:44.710
There's still a lot about the human mind

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:46.720
that we don't understand.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.970
And even the leaders of the
field of neurotechnology

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:52.433
are certainly a long way
from cracking its code.

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:56.730
Still, they've been able
to leverage what we do know

00:20:56.730 --> 00:21:00.060
into the beginnings of a
meaningful mind-machine link.

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:03.570
And it's clear the applications
will be incredibly varied,

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:05.563
and for some, life changing.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.930
As to what the future holds...

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:10.660
I think that we're really gonna see

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:13.940
kind of an integration
of kind of our brains

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:16.490
and, you know, machines for,

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:19.220
I think, really kind of
a profound alteration

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:21.100
of our human evolution, quite frankly.

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:23.980
Is it possible to create,
you know, virtual memories?

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:25.530
Absolutely, it is.

00:21:25.530 --> 00:21:28.000
Is it possible to have brain
to brain communication?

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:28.833
That's been, you know,

00:21:28.833 --> 00:21:31.830
demonstrated by some
researchers down at Duke.

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:35.383
Can you upload and download
information into the brain?

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:38.440
I think that's a theoretic possibility.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:40.750
Whether, again, that's
in 20 years or 100 years,

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:44.060
I think those are
possibilities going forward.

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:45.870
And I think just in the last century,

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:47.490
how we saw an explosive transition

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:49.340
from the Wright brothers' airplane

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:51.420
to kind of an F-22 Raptor,

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:53.620
which would have been
unimaginable 100 years ago,

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:54.900
we're gonna see that same evolution

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:56.450
with brain-computer interfaces.

